      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Heat oil in a pot over medium heat. Add mustard seeds and cumin seeds. When the mustard seeds pop, add curry leaves."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add 1 finely sliced onion. Cook 10 minutes until golden. Add garlic and ginger. Cook 2 minutes."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add tomato, turmeric, coriander, and chilli powder. Cook 8 minutes, stirring, until the tomato is pulped and the oil separates."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add the drained black-eyed beans, coconut milk, tamarind paste, and 300 ml water. Bring to a simmer."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Cook 20 minutes until the beans are well coated and the gravy is thick. Add garam masala and salt. Adjust water for a slightly soupy consistency."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Serve in bowls. Top with chopped onion, coriander, and peanuts. Serve with bread rolls on the side."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "Curry is too thick and dry",
              "cause": "Too much water evaporated during the cook",
              "fix": "Add 100 ml boiling water and stir well. Misal should be slightly brothy."
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "Lacks heat",
              "cause": "Chilli powder was reduced too much",
              "fix": "Stir in a teaspoon of chilli powder, cook 2 minutes, and taste again."
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Misal pav is eaten across Maharashtra for breakfast and as a mid-morning snack. Each city has its own style. Pune misal is milder and uses a dry garnish. Kolhapur misal is scorchingly hot. Nashik misal uses a more coconut-forward gravy. In Mumbai, the dish is eaten at small restaurants called khanavals from early morning. The pav used is the same Portuguese-origin soft roll found across Mumbai street food."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
